      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sandy earned its name from Brigham Young's observation of its thirsty soil, though historians find no evidence for that claim.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy%2C_Utah",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1873 railroad linked Sandy to Salt Lake City, sparking ore transport and turning the town into a mining supply hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy%2C_Utah",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sandy hosts four Blue Line TRAX stations, from Historic Sandy to Crescent View, weaving the city into the regional transit network.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy%2C_Utah",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The city is building a walkable center called The Cairns, stretching between 9000 and 11400 South along I‑15.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy%2C_Utah",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "When silver mining surged in Little Cottonwood Canyon, Sandy quickly became a bustling supply station with hotels, saloons, and brothels.",
